WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.520
A nematode and an earthworm look almost the same.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.900
Are they close relatives?

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.140
Both are soft, tube-shaped worms.

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.880
Each has a gut that runs from mouth to anus.

5
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:19.023
Slice each worm across the middle.

6
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:22.243
Add a third worm, a flat planarian.

7
00:00:23.563 --> 00:00:24.903
Now colour the tissue layers.

8
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:27.423
The outer layer is blue.

9
00:00:28.603 --> 00:00:30.623
The middle layer, mesoderm, is orange.

10
00:00:32.023 --> 00:00:35.323
It makes muscle. The gut lining is yellow.

11
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:40.747
The small white space in each centre is the gut lumen, where food goes.

12
00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:43.347
It is not the body cavity.

13
00:00:44.627 --> 00:00:48.547
The body cavity, if there is one, lies between gut and body wall.

14
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:52.227
In the planarian, tissue fills that space.

15
00:00:53.527 --> 00:00:54.747
There is no body cavity.

16
00:00:56.047 --> 00:01:00.507
In the nematode, a fluid space is lined by mesoderm only on the outside.

17
00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:03.047
That is a pseudocoelom.

18
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:08.477
In the earthworm, the fluid space is lined by mesoderm on both sides.

19
00:01:09.697 --> 00:01:10.957
That is a true coelom.

20
00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:14.437
Three worms, three ways to fill the same space.

21
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:20.117
Libbie Hyman's great survey of the invertebrates ordered animals this way.

22
00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:24.037
No cavity, then a partial lining, then a full one.

23
00:01:25.257 --> 00:01:27.917
It looks like a staircase, from simple to complex.

24
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.730
Now compare genes from many animals instead.

25
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.630
If the staircase were a family tree, where should the nematode land?

26
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.770
Between the flatworm and the earthworm, or somewhere else?

27
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:52.933
Somewhere else. The genes put the nematode with the arthropods, such as insects.

28
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.593
Both shed a tough outer cuticle as they grow.

29
00:01:57.853 --> 00:02:00.513
That moulting names the group, Ecdysozoa.

30
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.963
The planarian moves too.

31
00:02:04.263 --> 00:02:08.483
It lands beside the earthworm and a snail, in a group called Spiralia.

32
00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:11.223
Its relatives have a coelom.

33
00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:14.983
So the flatworm is not an early, simple branch.

34
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:18.373
Now mark each tip with its body cavity.

35
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:22.013
The cavity marks are scattered across the tree.

36
00:02:23.253 --> 00:02:25.673
The moulting mark sits on one branch only.

37
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:31.963
Animals that share a level of organization, like a body-cavity type, form a grade.

38
00:02:33.343 --> 00:02:34.483
A clade is something else.

39
00:02:35.723 --> 00:02:38.103
It is an ancestor and all of its descendants.

40
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:42.137
A shared, derived character can mark a clade.

41
00:02:43.337 --> 00:02:46.257
It is a new state that arose in a common ancestor.

42
00:02:47.797 --> 00:02:49.657
Moulting fits one origin on this tree.

43
00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:52.877
Cavity type changes several times.

44
00:02:54.057 --> 00:02:57.877
To test any character, lay it on a tree built from other evidence.

45
00:02:59.177 --> 00:03:00.757
Many genes are one such source.

46
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.460
So keep two jobs apart.

47
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.460
A body plan describes how an animal is built.

48
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.180
Independent evidence tests whom it descends from.
